  "body": "perf(kernel): instrument the step import stages (profile for #4200)\n\nInstrumentation and profile only. No behaviour change, no verdict change.\n\n## What changed\n\n- `crates/kernel/src/solidcore_step.rs`: `tracing::info!` timings with the\n  stable stage names `parse` (Part 21 parse, with progress milestones from\n  the `Watch` hook and the parse total), `topology` (unit/curve guards,\n  face-winding check, topology read) and `build` (per-solid build with one\n  line per solid, plus the loop total).\n- `crates/kernel/src/machining_mesh.rs`: `mesh_plan` (plan and the\n  judge/redivide pass), `mesh_deviation` (the sample measure), `facets`\n  (plan evaluation) and `weld` (vertex weld).\n- `crates/geometry/src/step.rs`: the kernel read total and one line per\n  solid meshed in the per-solid loop.\n- `crates/kernel/Cargo.toml`: `tracing` (a workspace dependency) for the\n  events, `tracing-subscriber` as a dev-dependency for the bench.\n- `crates/kernel/tests/mesh_deviation_bench.rs`: a new `#[ignore]`d\n  release-lane row `rotor_step_profile_bench` that reads the reporter's\n  rotor from `CAMCRAFT_ROTOR_STEP` and prints the read and mesh timings.\n  Without the variable set the row prints `rotor: skipped (set\n  CAMCRAFT_ROTOR_STEP)` and passes, so CI without it stays green.\n  Nothing pinned.\n- Log level: the per-parse progress lines and the per-stage and\n  per-attempt timing lines are `tracing::debug!` now, so the app's default\n  `info` filter no longer prints about 11 progress lines per STEP import\n  to stderr and the log. Reporters who want the lines set the filter to\n  `camcraft_kernel=debug` (`RUST_LOG=camcraft_kernel=debug`); the bench\n  sets its own filter to that level and still prints them.\n\n## Rotor profile (3,723,412 bytes, Windows x64, release)\n\nMeasured 2026-11-27, Windows x64, release. Rotor.STEP, one solid\n'Fillet2', 58 faces.\n\n| stage | time | note |\n|---|---|---|\n| `parse` | 33 ms | Part 21 parse of the 3.7 MB file |\n| `topology` (guards) | 3 ms | unit and curve checks |\n| `topology` (winding) | <1 ms | |\n| `topology` (read) | 16.5 s | kernel topology read and pcurve sewing |\n| `build` | 3.4 s | one solid, 58 faces |\n| read_step total | ~20 s | |\n| mesh at 0.005 mm: `mesh_plan` 64.0 s, judge/redivide 12.3 s, `mesh_deviation` 126.8 s; replan at 2.5e-3 mm: `mesh_plan` 37.2 s | ~240 s | refused: face 35 capped at 9.8e-3 mm sag |\n| mesh at 0.01 mm, attempt 1: `mesh_plan` 18.0 s, judge/redivide 2.4 s, `mesh_deviation` 167.4 s | ~188 s | rejected: measured 1.79e-2 mm over the 1e-2 mm tolerance |\n| mesh at 0.01 mm, attempt 2 (chord 5e-3): `mesh_plan` 64.8 s, judge/redivide 12.3 s, `mesh_deviation` 114.5 s, `facets` 0.85 s, `weld` 0.4 s | ~195 s | success: 5,830,496 triangles, judged 5.98e-3 mm |\n\nThe import loop of `crates/geometry/src/step.rs` runs the read (~20 s),\nthen a full 0.005 mm mesh attempt that ends refused (~240 s), then the\n0.01 mm loose-chord path (~380 s over its two attempts). That is about\n640 s of work before any mesh is handed out, so the 300 s watchdog\ncancels mid-way.\n\nWhere the time goes: `mesh_deviation` is the largest stage (115-167 s per\nattempt, 88-95 million samples over 5.8M triangles). `mesh_plan` is next\n(18-65 s per attempt, inside the pinned solidcore planner). The kernel\nread is 20 s of the 640 s.\n\n## Gate\n\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-kernel --test step_twisted_flank`: green (the\n  logging changed no verdict).\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-kernel --lib`: green (22 passed).\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-kernel -p camcraft-geometry --all-targets --\n  -D warnings`: clean.\n\n## Fix options for #4200 (from the profile)\n\n1. A seam-level pre-pass: measure the deviation per face as the plan is\n   built, so a plan that cannot meet the tolerance is refused before it\n   is fully made (the 126 s and 167 s measures of plans that were then\n   rejected or refused would shrink).\n2. Start at the edge-gap loose chord: the first full 0.005 mm attempt\n   (240 s) always ends refused for this file, and the 0.01 mm path then\n   replans to 5e-3 anyway.\n3. Raise the solidcore pin per `docs/decisions/916-solidcore-step-bench.md`\n   for the `topology` read and `mesh_plan` stages (36 s of 640 s; the\n   largest stage is our own `mesh_deviation` code, so the pin move alone\n   does not fix the bound).\n4. Parallel per-solid does not help here: the rotor is one solid.\n\nChangelog: none\n\nRefs #4200\n",
